        <div id="bike-or-pad" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When an Under-Desk Bike Beats a Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            A walking pad under a standing desk keeps you on your feet, which many people prefer,
            and burns more energy than seated pedalling at a similar effort. Our guide to <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-office/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for the office</a> covers that route. But a bike is the better choice in several common situations.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Your desk does not go high enough.</strong> A pad adds 4 to 6 inches under
            your feet, and many standing desks, and all fixed desks, cannot rise to match. An
            under-desk bike works at an ordinary sitting desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Your workplace will not allow a motorised machine.</strong> A pedal
            exerciser has no motor and needs no outlet. Its batteries run only the display.</li>
            <li><strong>Noise or neighbours.</strong> Footfall on a pad carries through floors. A
            magnetic pedal exerciser makes little more than a faint whir.</li>
            <li><strong>Balance, joints or fatigue.</strong> Seated pedalling takes body weight off
            the knees and hips, with no belt to step on or off. For many older users, or anyone
            with balance problems, that is the deciding factor.</li>
            <li><strong>Fine work.</strong> Most people write, draw and use a mouse more accurately
            sitting down. Pedalling slowly disturbs the upper body far less than walking.</li>
            <li><strong>Space and price.</strong> A pedal exerciser is about the size of a
            briefcase and costs a fraction of a desk and pad.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The trade-off is intensity and posture. You are still sitting, and pedalling at a
            gentle resistance burns less than walking. Think of an under-desk bike as a way to add
            light movement to sitting time, not a replacement for a proper workout. Our comparison
            of a <a href="/treadmill-vs-exercise-bike/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill and an exercise bike</a> covers the differences at full intensity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Knee Clearance and Pedal Height: Will It Fit Your Desk?</h2>
<p>
            The commonest problem with a pedal exerciser is knees hitting the underside of the desk
            at the top of each stroke. Whether that happens depends on three numbers: the height of
            the desk's underside, the height of your chair, and the pedal's highest point.
          </p>
          <p>
            MERACH's listing gives a simple version of the check: your calf length plus the bike's
            maximum pedal height should be no more than the desk clearance. Measure calf length
            from the floor to the top of your knee, sitting with your foot flat. Add the pedal
            height, 9.84 inches on the MERACH. If the total is more than the height of the desk's
            underside, your knees will hit it.
          </p>
          <p>
            DeskCycle addresses this directly. The DeskCycle 2 adjusts between 9 and 10 inches of
            pedal height, and DeskCycle says that at 9 inches it suits desks as low as 27 inches.
            Pedal exercisers that do not state a pedal height are a gamble on a low desk.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to make it fit</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sit further back.</strong> Placing the pedals further forward lowers the
            knees at the top of the stroke, at the cost of a longer reach to the keyboard.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower the chair</strong>, if your elbows will still be at keyboard
            height.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove a keyboard tray</strong>, which is often the lowest thing under the
            desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Raise the desk</strong>, if it is adjustable, and use a footrest when not
            pedalling.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stop the chair rolling away</h3>
          <p>
            Pedalling pushes your chair backwards on its castors. A chair without wheels, castor
            locks or cups, or a strap between the bike and the chair leg all help, and a non-slip
            mat under the bike stops it creeping forwards. Our under-desk elliptical guide covers
            this in more detail; a bike chair like the FlexiSpot V8 avoids the problem entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resistance-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Magnetic or Friction Resistance, and Noise</h2>
<p>
            Pedal exercisers use one of two kinds of resistance. <strong>Friction</strong> models
            press a pad or strap against the flywheel. They are cheap, but can be uneven, wear the
            pad and make a rubbing sound. <strong>Magnetic</strong> models slow the flywheel with
            magnets that never touch it. They are smoother, close to silent and do not wear, which
            is why every pedal exerciser on this page uses magnetic resistance or a belt drive
            described as quiet.
          </p>
          <p>
            Resistance settings vary. The DeskCycle 2 and MERACH give eight levels, the FlexiSpot V9
            eight, and the MagneTrainer and Sunny a stepless dial. At a desk, the useful range is
            the low end: enough resistance that the pedals do not spin away, light enough that you
            can keep a steady rhythm without your upper body moving or your breathing affecting
            your voice on a call. The higher levels are for dedicated sessions with the laptop
            closed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Flywheel feel matters too. DeskCycle says the DeskCycle 2's flywheel spins 14 times per
            pedal stroke, which gives the smooth, continuous feel of a real bike rather than a jerky
            push and pause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Displays, Apps and What the Numbers Mean</h2>
<p>
            Every bike here has a small display showing some combination of time, distance,
            speed, RPM and estimated calories. The MERACH links to MERACH's app; the Sunny's title
            calls it smart. A display is useful for one thing in particular: tracking time pedalled
            each day, which is the habit that matters.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treat distance and calorie figures loosely. Pedal exercisers usually estimate them from
            pedal revolutions with no knowledge of your weight or the actual resistance, so the
            numbers are rough. Use them to compare one day with the next, not to plan a diet. If
            you want a better estimate, a fitness tracker or heart-rate strap will do a better job;
            see our guide to <a href="/best-fitness-trackers-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">fitness trackers for walking pads</a>,
            most of which track cycling as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="using-at-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Using an Under-Desk Bike at Work</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Start low and slow.</strong> Pedal at the lowest resistance for 15 to 20
            minutes at a time and build up. Your shins and the fronts of your thighs will notice
            the new work.</li>
            <li><strong>Pedal through the right tasks.</strong> Reading, email, calls and
            meetings with your camera off suit pedalling. Pause for detailed work if your typing
            suffers.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind your posture.</strong> Sit upright and keep the keyboard close. If you
            find yourself perched on the front of the seat to reach the pedals, move the bike
            closer.</li>
            <li><strong>Wear suitable shoes.</strong> Most pedal exercisers have straps; a flat,
            closed shoe sits in them better than heels or sandals.</li>
            <li><strong>Check with your workplace.</strong> A pedal exerciser rarely raises
            objections, but a desk bike or bike chair takes floor space and may need approval.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Pedal exercisers are also widely used by older adults for gentle daily movement while
            watching television; if that is the main use, see our guide to <a href="/best-exercise-bikes-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">exercise bikes for seniors</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If your desk can rise high enough and you want to stand</strong>, a walking
            pad keeps you on your feet and moving more; pair it with a desk from our guide to <a href="/best-standing-desks-for-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standing desks for walking pads</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a smoother, gliding motion</strong> that is easier on the knees,
            an under-desk elliptical moves your feet in a flatter oval.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want real cardio sessions</strong>, a full exercise bike with a heavier
            flywheel and proper seat is far better; see our guide to <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">recumbent bikes</a> for a seated option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With Under-Desk Bikes</h2>
          <p>Most of these are about fit, which is the part of buying a pedal exerciser people skip.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Not measuring knee clearance</h3>
              <p>A pedal exerciser that lifts your knees into the underside of the desk is useless at work. Add calf length to the pedal height and compare it with the desk's underside before buying, and choose a model that states its pedal height.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Pedalling on a rolling chair with no fix</h3>
              <p>Every stroke pushes the chair back a little, until you are perched on the edge reaching for the pedals. Lock the castors, use a chair without wheels or strap the bike to the chair.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Setting the resistance too high</h3>
              <p>High resistance moves your upper body and changes your breathing, which affects typing and your voice on calls. Keep it low while working and save the higher settings for dedicated sessions.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Believing the calorie readout</h3>
              <p>Pedal exercisers estimate calories from pedal turns without knowing your weight or the true resistance. Track minutes pedalled instead, and use a fitness tracker if you want a better estimate.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most desk workers, the DeskCycle 2 is the pedal exerciser to
            buy:</strong> magnetic, smooth and adjustable in pedal height, with a stated fit for
            desks as low as 27 inches. The MERACH does much the same for less, with an app, and the
            Sunny SF-B023005 is a cheap way to see whether pedalling at a desk suits you.
          </p>
          <p>
            For arms as well as legs, the MagneTrainer; for a dedicated workstation, the FlexiSpot V9
            desk bike; and for pedalling at your own desk without a rolling chair, the FlexiSpot V8.
            If your desk can rise and you would rather stand, see our guide to <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-office/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">walking pads for the office</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

