        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is an under-desk bike as good as a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For energy burned, a walking pad usually does more, because you are on your feet. An under-desk bike is better when your desk cannot rise high enough, your workplace will not allow a motor, noise matters, or balance and joints make standing exercise difficult.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will an under-desk bike fit under my desk?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check by adding your calf length (floor to top of the knee, seated) to the bike's maximum pedal height. If the total is more than the height of the desk's underside, your knees will hit it. The DeskCycle 2 lists a 9 to 10 inch pedal height and says it fits desks as low as 27 inches.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are under-desk bikes quiet enough for meetings?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Magnetic models generally are, at low resistance. Friction models can rub and squeak. A non-slip mat under the bike also stops it knocking against the floor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I stop my chair rolling back when I pedal?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Use a chair without wheels, fit castor locks or cups, or strap the bike to a chair leg. A non-slip mat under the bike helps. A bike chair such as the FlexiSpot V8 avoids the problem because the pedals and seat are one unit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many calories does an under-desk bike burn?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on your weight, resistance and cadence, and the displays only estimate. At the gentle resistance most people use while working, expect light activity rather than a workout. Use the display to track time pedalled, which is the habit that matters.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between an under-desk bike and an under-desk elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A bike moves your feet in a circle, like cycling. An elliptical moves them in a flatter oval that many people find gentler on the knees and needs less knee lift. Bikes tend to give more resistance; ellipticals a smoother glide.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

